detail.abstract:
Alex Pentland's Shared Wisdom (MIT Press, 2025) celebrates emergent institutions while proposing designed "consensus networks" to achieve predetermined goals like the UN SDGs.&nbsp; 
<div>
 This paper identifies the resulting contradiction, which I term the Pentland Paradox: one cannot simultaneously endorse evolutionary epistemology and constructivist design. I formalize this critique through the Pentland Impossibility Theorem, proving that under dispersed knowledge and heterogeneous preferences, no designed coordination mechanism can simultaneously satisfy participation constraints, incentive compatibility, and goal achievement without coercion.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Three empirical cases validate the theorem: UK Data Trusts (0% operational deployment despite £2M+ funding), EU ETS Phase I (99.7% price collapse from over-allocation), and GDPR (20% increased data costs with no measurable privacy improvement). Drawing on Mises, Hayek, Darwin, and Dennett, I propose an Extended Phenotype Theory alternative: legal institutions are cultural replicators subject to selection pressures, not artifacts of intelligent design.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The evolutionarily stable alternative is Pure Evolutionary Governance: no predetermined goals, ex-post liability only, federalism enabling jurisdictional competition, and negative selection (pruning failures) rather than positive design. Steel-manning five defenses Pentland might offer, I show each collapses: "bottom-up design" conflates voluntary coordination with mandates; "irreversibility" arguments prove consensus networks are 26× slower than evolutionary adaptation; "democratic legitimacy" ignores that markets aggregate billions of daily preference revelations versus periodic elections.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The choice is stark: evolution or engineering, not both.
</div>
